course: dictionary
lesson: 11951
title: Cardiac and the heart
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Cardiac is an adjective, a describing word, and it means to do with the heart. Use context to work it out: «The doctor listened to his cardiac rhythm with a stethoscope.» A stethoscope is used to listen to the heart, so cardiac must be about the heart.
explain: «She is training to be a cardiac surgeon.» A surgeon is a doctor who operates on people, so a cardiac surgeon works on hearts. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then swap in your meaning, "heart", and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Cardiac comes from a Greek word for heart, so when you see it, think of your heart beating. Words like this often appear in health and science lessons, so learning one helps you understand the others.
fact: Cardiac means to do with the heart.
fact: «Cardiac arrest» means the heart suddenly stops.
fact: If someone has a cardiac arrest, call an ambulance, start CPR and use a defibrillator if one is nearby.
fact: «Cardiac muscle» is the heart muscle, which keeps the heart beating.
fact: Other phrases with cardiac include «cardiac surgeon», a doctor who operates on hearts.
exercise: «The doctor checked the patient's cardiac health.» What does cardiac mean? | To do with the head | *To do with the heart | To do with the feet | To do with the teeth
exercise: «She is training to be a cardiac surgeon.» Who does a cardiac surgeon work on? | Teeth | Feet | *Hearts | Eyes
exercise: «The nurse said, "Call an ambulance, this is a cardiac arrest!"» What stops in a cardiac arrest? | *The heart | The ears | The hair | The thumb
test: «Cardiac muscle keeps your heart beating.» What is cardiac muscle? | *The heart muscle, which makes the heart beat | A muscle in your legs | A muscle in your eyes | A muscle in your arms
test: «Ravi's grandad had a cardiac arrest at the park.» What does cardiac arrest mean? | *The heart suddenly stops | A sore throat | A broken arm | A loud noise
exam: Which word in this sentence means to do with the heart? «The cardiac team worked fast to save the man.» | team | worked | save | *cardiac
